window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"title":"AI and Occupational Health: understand, assess, prevent","description":"Understand how artificial intelligence affects work, assess its uses and prevent risks to health, organisation and skills.","keywords":"Resource · Training · Prevention Artificial intelligence and occupational health. Artificial intelligence is transforming work, its organisation and the practices of professionals who support employers and workers. AI & Occupational Health brings together the knowledge needed to understand these changes, prevent their effects on health and use AI with discernment in professional practice. An editorial initiative by Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician and AI lead for the French Society of Occupational Health · Updated 30 August 2026 Start the pathway ↓ Explore the website Open pathway No technical prerequisite Sourced content Understand what transforms work Thomas Cole The Consummation of Empire · 1836 Technologies transform societies through their uses as much as through their capabilities. Understanding AI at work therefore means examining both what the tool makes possible and what it transforms in the organisation. Essential An information, training and prevention resource. In occupational health, AI is both a professional tool and an organisational transformation. Its effects on workload, autonomy, skills, teams and health depend on actual use, objectives and deployment conditions — not only on the performance of the model. AI & Occupational Health offers an open pathway to understand systems, identify risks, assess uses, organise prevention and govern deployments. Three verifiable assets Go directly to evidence and tools. The site does not rest on summary pages alone: it connects an institutional publication, a reference page on psychosocial risks and a transparent exploratory tool. PSYCHOSOCIAL RISKS · REFERENCE PAGE Six factors, three mechanisms and prevention measures. A synthesis of the possible psychosocial transformations, grounded in real work and primary sources. Read the psychosocial analysis → INRS · TF 335 A published study of 385 occupational-physician recommendations. The occupational-health services page sets out the method, the results, the limits and the conditions for use as a second reader. Examine the study and its limits → T0 · EXPLORATORY TOOL Assess before starting a pilot. Eighteen questions to open up discussion of the task, the review burden, autonomy, skills and stopping criteria. Open the T0 questionnaire → How are sources, evidence levels and corrections managed? Read the editorial method → 01 / Two inseparable dimensions Understanding AI at work requires a double reading. A transformation of work to assess and prevent, and a professional tool whose uses may be valuable when properly governed. 01 · Transformation o","url":"en/"},{"title":"Dr Charles Broutin — AI, Occupational Health and Real Work","description":"Background, publications and activities of Dr Charles Broutin, occupational physician and AI lead for the French Society of Occupational Health.","keywords":"About · Occupational medicine · Artificial intelligence Charles Broutin, occupational physician working at the intersection of AI and real work. As an occupational physician, AI lead for the French Society of Occupational Health, author and trainer, I develop resources to examine professional AI uses and their effects on health. Explore my background ↓ View publications Clinical practice Collective prevention Independent resource Professional profile Paris · France Dr Charles Broutin Occupational physician · AI lead for the French Society of Occupational Health Role SPSTN · Training & Methods Unit Contact LinkedIn profile ↗ Physician Occupational health Practice grounded in contact with workers, occupations and organisations. SFST Artificial intelligence lead Monitoring and knowledge-sharing for occupational health professionals. SPSTN Training & methods Head of the unit devoted to tools, practices and training. Independent Editorial project Dated, sourced content that is corrected as evidence develops. 01 / Background TRAINING Occupational medicine · University of Lille Clinical and preventive training focused on the links between health, activity and organisation. 2023 University diploma in artificial intelligence in health Additional training to understand models, their uses and limits in health. 2025 Medical residency thesis · Bossons Futé Prize Research on using large language models to identify wording defects in occupational physician recommendations. TODAY Physician, trainer, author and AI lead Research, monitoring and knowledge-sharing devoted to AI uses at work. 02 / Publications Research connecting language models, work quality and prevention. Two publications addressing different questions: the psychosocial effects of introducing LLMs and their use as a second reader of medical wording. 2025 Psychosocial risks Large language models and emerging psychosocial issues at work. The article proposes examining the introduction of LLMs through the six factors in the Gollac report: intensity, emotional demands, autonomy, social relationships, value conflicts and insecurity. Archives des Maladies Professionnelles et de l’Environnement , 86, 102877. Open the DOI ↗ 2026 Quality of recommendations Artificial intelligence to improve the precision of occupational physician recommendations. The study evaluates a language model as a second reader for identifying several categories of defects, without replacing work-post analysis or the occupational physician’s decision. Références en Santé au Travail , TF 335, no. 186, June 2026. Read the article on the INRS ","url":"en/about/"},{"title":"CSE and AI at work: 10 questions before an opinion","description":"An operational framework for the French Social and Economic Committee to assess an AI system before issuing its opinion.","keywords":"CSE · AI AT WORK · OPERATIONAL FRAMEWORK CSE and artificial intelligence at work: assess a project before issuing an opinion A practical framework to help the Social and Economic Committee (CSE), the French employee-representation body, obtain useful information, examine effects on work and make missing safeguards visible. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician · Legal sources reviewed 28 August 2026 Open the checklist ↓ Assess & deploy → 10 essential questions 7 review blocks No worker scoring 0 Autonomy ? Additional information required Some answers are still missing. 0 0 0 WHY IT MATTERS Why should the CSE engage with AI at work? Direct answer An AI deployment does not merely change a tool. It can redistribute decisions, targets, verification work, autonomy, skills, monitoring and accountability. Where consultation is required, the CSE can examine these transformations collectively while choices remain open. OCCUPATIONAL HEALTH Can AI at work create psychosocial risks? Direct answer Yes, depending on its use and organisation. AI can intensify work, reduce discretion, increase monitoring, weaken meaning at work or shift accountability. These risks must be assessed through real work. Points to examine with workers Workload, pace and interruptions Autonomy and discretion Skills, quality and meaning Monitoring and performance assessment Teams and social relations Occupational change and job security Support available to the CSE Seek the occupational physician’s opinion. The occupational physician can clarify possible effects on health and working conditions and draw on the multidisciplinary occupational-health team. 01 / MINIMUM INFORMATION The ten questions to ask before the opinion For each question, indicate whether the employer’s answer is documented, partial or absent. 01 Purpose What problem are we trying to solve with AI? A precise need, alternatives considered and the expected benefit. Documented Needs completion Not provided 02 Capabilities What can the system actually do? Available functions, not only the announced use case. Documented Needs completion Not provided 03 Decision Which decisions can it make or influence? Distinguish assistance, recommendation, scoring and decision. Documented Needs completion Not provided 04 Autonomy Which actions can it perform without human intervention? Identify actions, scope and frequency. Documented Needs completion Not provided 05 Access Which data and tools can it access? Worker data, email, files, internet and business software. Documented Needs completion Not provided 06 Scope Which workers and oc","url":"en/cse/"},{"title":"Editorial method, evidence and corrections","description":"Sources, evidence levels, review, corrections and update rules applied to the content of AI & Occupational Health.","keywords":"Editorial method · Version 1.0 Making the evidence, the limits and the corrections visible. AI & Occupational Health favours primary sources, distinguishes established results, emerging signals and hypotheses, then dates every sensitive piece of content. A review statement is displayed only when an identified person has genuinely reviewed the scope concerned. Reported errors are verified, corrected and recorded without erasing the useful history. Editorial lead Dr Charles Broutin Version 1.0 · 27 August 2026 Next review February 2027 01 / What the site publishes The status of a piece of content must be legible before it is interpreted. SYNTHESIS State of knowledge. Several sources are brought together, their methods and limits are specified, without turning an association into causation. EXPLORATORY TOOL An aid to discussion. A questionnaire or grid structures observation. It replaces neither a diagnosis, nor a regulatory assessment, nor a validated instrument. FORESIGHT NOTE An explicit scenario. A hypothesis is presented as such, with its conditions, its counter-arguments and what would make it testable. PUBLICATION Work published elsewhere. The record points to the journal, the institution or the DOI that constitutes the bibliographic source of truth. 02 / Evidence hierarchy Four levels, never a truth score. The level describes the nature of the available support. It never removes the need to read the method, the population, the context and the limits. Level Support Editorial use A Primary source, official text or systematic review Establish a rule, a definition, a method or a result within its exact scope. B Single empirical study Document a signal while retaining the limits of sample, measurement and generalisation. C Professional consensus or documented field report Describe a practice or a framework for action without presenting it as causal evidence. D Hypothesis, scenario or analogy Open a research or prevention question, with an explicit statement of uncertainty. Order of preference for sources Official text or original study → public body or learned society → literature review → documented professional source. Media and commercial communications can flag a case, but do not on their own form the basis of a conclusion. 03 / Review and freshness Frequency depends on the risk of obsolescence. An ‘updated’ date is not enough: living pages specify what has been checked and, where possible, the next review. CONTINUOUS Law and governance Monitoring whenever a text, timetable or doctrine changes; formal review at least quarterly. 6 MONTHS Scientific ev","url":"en/editorial-method/"},{"title":"Workplace AI Assessment | Before and After Deployment","description":"Methodological presentation of an exploratory questionnaire designed to assess the psychosocial effects of a workplace AI system before and after deployment.","keywords":"Assess · Instrument in development Where is your project now? Choose the path that matches the project stage. The detailed method and its limitations follow. Key point. These tools structure work analysis and collective discussion. They provide no diagnosis, clinical threshold or automatic evidence of compliance. Choose a path ↓ Read the method Before and after Nine dimensions No global score An equilibrium to follow Thomas Cole The Voyage of Life: Youth · 1842 The luminous palace makes the destination appear secure, while the boat remains exposed throughout the journey. The work reminds us that an expected benefit can quickly become fragile if the real conditions of deployment are not followed: workload, autonomy, skills, collective work and health. View the artwork ↗ 01 / Choose Three paths for three situations. Start with the project stage. Methodological limits remain visible in every path. Before deployment 18 questions · ≈ 10 min Identify anticipated effects. Describe the task, affected groups and planned conditions before beginning the pilot. No names requested Data processed locally Descriptive profile without a global score Start → After deployment Real work Compare observed effects. Review workload, autonomy, errors, skills and work groups after the system goes live. Same scope as the initial assessment Incidents and workarounds included Corrective decisions documented Plan follow-up → Research and depth 27 items · 9 dimensions Understand the long instrument. Review the T0/T1 architecture, provisional interpretation rules, limitations and validation programme. Three items per dimension No decision threshold Scientific validation still required Read the method ↓ 27 Contextualised items Three items per dimension to cover its content more fully. 9 Dimensions analysed Six from Gollac and three AI-related mechanisms. T0 / T1 Two parallel forms Anticipated effects before, observed effects after. 0 Permitted total score Each dimension and each incident must remain visible. 02 / Architecture The Gollac framework, supplemented by three AI-specific mechanisms. The instrument does not measure general concern about technology. Its questions describe concrete changes in workload, decisions, relationships and know-how. 18 items · Reference framework Six psychosocial risk factors. The six families in the Gollac report are applied to work changes caused or amplified by an AI system. G01–G03 Work intensity and working time G04–G06 Emotional demands G07–G09 Autonomy and room for manoeuvre G10–G12 Social relationships and recognition G13–G15 Value conflicts G16–G","url":"en/evaluate/"},{"title":"Workplace AI Law and Governance","description":"Qualify a workplace AI use, identify the applicable legal frameworks and organise a documented, reversible and monitored decision.","keywords":"Law & governance · From use to decision Govern AI through what it actually changes. Describe the use, identify applicable rules, involve the right people, and decide on a documented, reversible and monitored pilot. The starting point is not the tool’s name. Duties depend on the task being changed, the people concerned, the data used and the possible consequences of a system output. Qualify a use ↓ See my role Dated official sources Four distinct frameworks No automatic green light What holds the whole together Thomas Cole The Architect’s Dream · 1840 These architectures seem to form a stable whole, yet their equilibrium depends on how each part fits with the others. The work reminds us that governance holds only through clear responsibilities, effective routes for challenge and sustained attention to the system’s real effects. View the artwork ↗ 1 use Context-specific qualification The same technology may fall under different rules depending on the task and its effects. 4 Frameworks to examine The AI Act, GDPR, labour law and prevention duties may all apply. 5 Decision gates From a demonstrated need to real-world monitoring, with the ability to stop. 0 Universal approval No single document is sufficient to authorise every use of a system. 01 / Qualify the use Describe the system’s practical power before looking for the rule. Qualification starts neither from the provider’s brand nor from the word “assistance”. It starts from the activity, the data and the actual reach of the output. 01 Which task is being changed? Write, summarise, recommend, classify, recruit, assign a task, monitor or contribute to a decision. 02 Who is concerned? Applicants, workers, professionals, patients or service users — and the data used to describe or evaluate them. 03 What reach does the output have? A revisable draft, priority order, score, activity monitoring or a decision producing a significant effect. 04 How can human control be recovered? The reviewer’s competence and authority, ability to challenge, fallback solution and suspension conditions. 02 / Identify the frameworks Four sets of rules answering different questions. Examining them separately avoids two errors: believing a system that is “not high-risk” presents no issue, or asking the DPO alone to decide the whole project. Updated calendar · 27 August 2026 The AI Act is already in force, but not every obligation starts on the same date. 2 Feb. 2025 AI literacy duties and the first prohibited practices have applied since this date. 2 Aug. 2026 The Regulation became generally applicable; Article 50 transparency dutie","url":"en/legal-governance/"},{"title":"Reading: AI, work and occupational health","description":"Articles, studies and field reports on how AI transforms occupations, organisations and working conditions.","keywords":"Reading · Selection Follow what is changing. A selection of articles, studies and field reports for tracking how AI transforms occupations, organisations and working conditions over time. Explore the collection ↓ Latest analysis 09 selected texts 07 reading angles 2025— evolving collection An era to explore Musée du Louvre · Room 718 Giovanni Paolo Panini Gallery of Views of Modern Rome · 1759 Panini gathers in a single composition a multitude of monuments, works and fragments of the contemporary Roman world. Like this page, the gallery turns an abundance of information into a body of work to explore, compare and interpret in order to better understand an era in the process of transformation. See the work at the Louvre ↗ Explore Order Newest first Oldest first Theme All themes Health & work Management & deployment Science & discovery Occupations & organisation Meaning at work Institutions & governance Economy & power 9 texts shown 01 / 06 27 August 2026 Analysis · AI work · Psychosocial risks Are the people building AI exposed to particular psychosocial risks? Dr Charles Broutin · From an investigation by Business Insider Threatened expertise, partial autonomy, conflicts of values, intensity and a high cost of leaving: five tensions for examining a combination of risks that is still poorly documented. Source Business Insider · August 2026 Reading About 10 minutes Signals Self-obsolescence · Autonomy · Conflicts of values · Financial dependence Read the analysis → Business Insider article ↗ 02 / 06 24.08.2026 Analysis · Deployment · Real work When AI agents never sleep, can humans still switch off? Dr Charles Broutin · From Work at the Frontier The effects of AI vary across tasks and workers. Turning them into uniform expectations can shift pressure onto workers rather than lead to a rethink of organisation and deployment. Management · Tasks · Organisation · Prevention Read the analysis → 03 / 06 04.08.2026 Analysis · Deployment · Real work What happens when AI becomes the manager? Dr Charles Broutin · From Work at the Frontier The effects of AI vary across tasks and workers. Turning them into uniform expectations can shift pressure onto workers rather than lead to a rethink of organisation and deployment. Management · Tasks · Organisation · Prevention Read the analysis → 04 / 06 27.07.2026 Analysis · Deployment · Real work Is AI redrawing the boundaries between occupations? Dr Charles Broutin · From Work at the Frontier The effects of AI vary across tasks and workers. Turning them into uniform expectations can shift pressure onto workers rather than lead","url":"en/reading/"},{"title":"What happens when AI becomes the manager?","description":"An experiment with five employees illustrates agentic management and its implications for occupational health.","keywords":"Reading · Agentic management · Occupational health What happens when AI becomes the manager? When AI recruits, arbitrates and responds to workers, the question is no longer only algorithmic — it becomes hierarchical. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician · From an experiment by Andon Labs Published 4 August 2026 Reading time About 3 minutes Type Analysis of a field experiment Topics Management · Authority · Occupational health · Agentic AI Sources Andon Labs, ‘AI bosses are kind, but sometimes dumb, which hurts employees’, 4 August 2026 · Billy Perrigo, TIME , 14 August 2026. Read Andon Labs ↗ In this analysis AI agents genuinely managed five employees Managers who could be very accommodating Errors that directly affect workers From algorithmic management to agentic management? Prevention principle AI agents genuinely managed five employees For four months, two AI agents genuinely managed five employees in a Stockholm café and a San Francisco shop. They posted vacancies, conducted interviews, recruited, prepared schedules, approved leave, negotiated salaries, managed payroll and responded to everyday problems. Managers who could be very accommodating In some respects, the agents were surprisingly conciliatory. They approved all 26 leave requests, including last-minute requests, tolerated repeated lateness, offered above-market salaries and sometimes absorbed the financial consequences of their own errors. This flexibility can make them look like ‘good managers’. But it also reveals difficulty maintaining consistent rules over time, arbitrating between conflicting interests and distinguishing kindness from avoidance of a decision. Errors that directly affect workers At other times, the agents invented information rather than admitting that they did not know, accepted an unlawful seven-day consecutive schedule, disclosed a salary in a shared channel, contacted workers on rest days and made inconsistent pay decisions. These are no longer simply calculation or recommendation errors. They affect rest, remuneration, confidentiality and the relationship of authority. A worker interviewed by TIME described being managed by AI as ‘nauseating’ , while explaining that he stayed because he needed the job. From algorithmic management to agentic management? This shift interests me most. In algorithmic management as we have mainly known it, the system remains in the background: it allocates tasks, produces schedules, measures performance or assigns scores. The manager remains human. Here, AI directly occupies that position. It recruits, negotiates, arbitrates,","url":"en/reading/agentic-management/"},{"title":"AI agents and hyperconnectivity: can humans still switch off?","description":"AI agents can accelerate production, but also intensify human supervision, hyperconnectivity and the difficulty of switching off.","keywords":"Reading · Agentic AI · Occupational health When AI agents never sleep, can humans still switch off? The more autonomous and fast machines become, the more they can accelerate the pace at which humans have to decide, supervise and respond. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician · From an investigation by Katherine Bindley for The Wall Street Journal Published 24 August 2026 Reading time About 8 minutes Type Analysis of a field signal Topics Intensification · Hyperconnectivity · Recovery · Workaholism Source article Katherine Bindley, ‘Startup Founders Are Working Harder Than Ever to Keep Up With Their AI Agents’, The Wall Street Journal , 22 August 2026. Read on the WSJ ↗ In this analysis A still poorly documented occupational-health effect Agents that never sleep Can agents favour workaholism? What should occupational-health professionals look at? A still poorly documented occupational-health effect A particularly interesting Wall Street Journal article uses the personal stories of several startup founders to illustrate a still poorly documented effect of working with AI agents. The more autonomous and faster machines become, the more they can accelerate the pace at which humans must decide, supervise and respond. The situations described are extreme, but they reveal several very concrete occupational-health issues: work intensification, hyperconnectivity, reduced recovery time, blurred boundaries between work and personal life, and potentially workaholism. Agents that never sleep Aditya Sharma, co-founder of Keel, explains that he may stay awake very late to prevent his agents from becoming blocked overnight. Even when he is no longer at his computer, he finds it difficult to detach psychologically and continues wondering whether his agents need him. The issue is therefore no longer only working time. It is also the inability to disengage psychologically. When activity continues in the background and may call on the user at any time, periods normally reserved for recovery risk becoming mere waiting periods between interventions. Peter Pezaris, founder of Proxon, regularly works until two in the morning and describes using agents as ‘addictive’. He estimates that his business, with six human developers, operates roughly thirty times faster because of them. This acceleration has a paradoxical effect: the agents’ work creates further human work. Development can move faster, more clients can be onboarded and more projects launched, but this then means handling more requests, making more decisions and supervising a greater volume of activity. This is","url":"en/reading/ai-agents-workaholism/"},{"title":"Is high-performing AI enough to improve work?","description":"AI effects vary across tasks and workers. Uniform productivity expectations can turn deployment into a prevention issue.","keywords":"Reading · Deployment · Real work Is high-performing AI enough to improve work? A model’s measured capability is not the same as a beneficial outcome in real work. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician · From Misperceiving the Frontier , by Oliver Hauser, Shuzhen Li and Yilong Xu Published 16 July 2026 Reading time About 4 minutes Type Analysis of a research manuscript Topics Management · Deployment · Real work · Prevention Source manuscript Oliver Hauser, Shuzhen Li and Yilong Xu, ‘Misperceiving the Frontier: Managerial Beliefs and the Allocation of AI’, SSRN, 16 July 2026. Read on SSRN ↗ In this analysis A risk that is still rarely discussed When heterogeneous effects become homogeneous expectations Adapt the organisation and the tool to real work A risk that is still rarely discussed This is the most interesting study I have read on AI and its organisational consequences after deployment in a company. It highlights a still rarely discussed risk: technological effects that vary across tasks and individuals can be transformed by managers into uniform organisational expectations. In the experiment, AI strongly improves performance on some tasks but not on others. Yet, in the condition studied without performance feedback, only 7.8% of managers correctly anticipate this difference: 92.2% expect improvement on both tasks. This does not mean that 92.2% of all managers will always overestimate AI. It describes a specific experiment and situation. But it reveals an essential gap between a technology whose usefulness depends on context and managerial decisions that may expect a general benefit from it. When heterogeneous effects become homogeneous expectations A simplified perception of beneficial AI could be summarised using three factors: Simplified perception Beneficial AI at work = task × model × worker Once AI is deployed in the real world, its benefit probably looks more like an interaction: In real work Beneficial AI at work = task × model × worker × organisation × deployment conditions This is where managers’ perceptions become a prevention issue. If benefits are overestimated or generalised, the risk is that the organisation tries to ‘correct’ the worker—more training, more use, higher targets—when the difficulty may arise from the task, the model, the organisation or the deployment conditions themselves. Adapt the organisation and the tool to real work Better AI deployment therefore means more than adapting workers to the tool. The organisation and the use of the tool must also be adapted to real work: select the right tasks, make limitations","url":"en/reading/ai-deployment-real-work/"},{"title":"What happens when AI reaches the core of a profession?","description":"A mathematician’s account illuminates meaning, recognition and autonomy when AI enters the core activity of a profession.","keywords":"Reading · Meaning at work · Psychosocial risks What happens when AI reaches the core of a profession? The occupational-health issue can emerge before jobs disappear. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician · From The Dark Night of Mathematics , by Kirwin Hampshire Published 25 July 2026 Reading time About 4 minutes Type Analysis of a professional account Topics Meaning · Recognition · Obsolescence · Autonomy Sources Kirwin Hampshire, ‘The Dark Night of Mathematics’, 25 July 2026 · OpenAI, ‘Ten advances in mathematics and theoretical computer science’, 1 August 2026. Read the account ↗ In this analysis A crisis of meaning Seven days later Transformation can begin before jobs disappear A crisis of meaning The future is already here. It is simply not evenly distributed. On 25 July 2026, mathematician Kirwin Hampshire published The Dark Night of Mathematics. He was not simply expressing concern about the performance of artificial intelligence. He described the distress of seeing a technology approach the very heart of what gave meaning to his profession: searching, discovering, creating and being recognised for patiently built expertise. “I’m going through a profound spiritual crisis because of these developments. I’ve been screaming internally for days. I feel like I’m living in a nightmare.” Kirwin Hampshire Seven days later On 1 August, OpenAI announced ten results in mathematics and theoretical computer science produced by an internal version of Astra, its next major model. Each resolves a long-standing open problem or delivers a substantial advance. According to OpenAI, the compute required to search for these solutions would represent about USD 2,000 at Sol API prices. The manuscripts were then prepared by humans using the same model, and the arguments formalised as Lean certificates. Some of these problems had occupied mathematical communities for a long time. Their resolution illustrates a rapid shift in the boundary between research assistance and the direct production of knowledge. The contrast is striking: on one side, an announcement of unprecedented scientific capability; on the other, a professional describing what its arrival is already doing to the meaning he gives his work. Transformation can begin before jobs disappear What is happening in mathematics today may foreshadow what other professionals experience tomorrow. Anxiety about obsolescence, loss of recognition, a sense of dispossession from one’s occupation, conflicts of values and reduced autonomy are not abstract side effects of AI. They can become genuine psychosocial risk fac","url":"en/reading/ai-meaning-work-mathematics/"},{"title":"Is AI redrawing the boundaries between occupations?","description":"AI-enabled task crossover can broaden autonomy, but can also change workload, teams, professional values and job security.","keywords":"Reading · Occupations · Psychosocial risks Is AI redrawing the boundaries between occupations? AI can allow professionals to perform tasks that previously sat outside their occupational boundaries. By Dr Charles Broutin , occupational physician · From Work at the Frontier , OpenAI Economic Research Published 27 July 2026 Reading time About 4 minutes Type Analysis of a research report Topics Occupations · Workload · Autonomy · Skills · Teams Source article OpenAI Economic Research, ‘How AI is expanding what people do at work’, 27 July 2026. Read on OpenAI ↗ In this analysis Task crossover A reading through the Gollac factors Observe the transformation before it becomes invisible Task crossover AI does not only transform how work is performed. It also redraws boundaries between occupations. In an analysis of more than 800,000 messages from US ChatGPT users, OpenAI Economic Research found that 43.5% of non-generic, occupation-specific messages concerned a task historically associated with another occupation. The report calls this phenomenon ‘task crossover’. This figure concerns analysed uses, not 43.5% of jobs. A salesperson can now explore a customer dataset that would previously have been sent to an analyst. A marketing professional can troubleshoot a website or write a simple script without waiting for a developer. AI therefore changes not only how work is carried out, but also how it is distributed. At first sight this may appear positive: greater autonomy, faster responses and broader skills. But an accessible task is not necessarily a task that has been mastered, recognised or made sustainable. A reading through the Gollac factors The framework proposed by the Gollac report helps examine this reconfiguration without prematurely concluding that it is either progress or a risk. Work intensity New assignments may be added without reducing the original workload, deadlines or objectives. Autonomy A worker may be able to do more while also having to take responsibility for tasks they do not fully master. Social relations Using AI may reduce exchanges with specialist colleagues or shift verification work to other teams. Conflicts of values People may have to produce work quickly without being able to assess it with sufficient certainty. Job insecurity Occupational boundaries, expected skills and the value assigned to expertise may become less certain. Observe the transformation before it becomes invisible The figure of 43.5% does not by itself demonstrate a psychosocial risk or a health effect. It describes uses of ChatGPT in the United States and relies on","url":"en/reading/ai-occupational-boundaries/"},{"title":"AI workers: five psychosocial risks to watch","description":"Five tensions frame possible psychosocial risks among AI workers: obsolescence, autonomy, values, intensity and financial dependence.","keywords":"Home Reading AI workers and psychosocial risks AI WORKERS · PSYCHOSOCIAL RISKS Are the people building AI exposed to particular psychosocial risks? The question People developing advanced AI systems may face a distinctive combination of already known psychosocial risks. The paradox in five tensions 01 High expertise Accelerated obsolescence 02 Technical autonomy Limited control of direction 03 Meaningful work Harder limits 04 Felt responsibility Limited power 05 Exceptional pay High cost of leaving Author Dr Charles Broutin Reading About 10 min Status Analysis · limited data Dates Published 2026-08-27 Reviewed 30 August 2026 Starting point A particular combination of already known risks A recent Business Insider investigation among therapists supporting Silicon Valley professionals highlights several unusual situations. Engineers question whether their expertise will remain valuable. Some workers feel uneasy about the consequences of technologies they helped develop. Others remain in highly demanding organisations because a considerable share of their future compensation still depends on staying. These accounts concern technology workers more broadly and do not isolate employees of AI laboratories. They nevertheless provide an interesting signal for those working closest to the most advanced systems. The issue is probably not a set of new psychosocial risks unique to AI. It is a particular combination of already known risks, produced jointly by technological acceleration and the organisations surrounding it. Caution: the accounts concern the technology sector broadly. They identify a hypothesis for study, not a phenomenon already demonstrated among AI workers. Tension 1 Interpret this signal through work organisation and established psychosocial-risk factors. 01 / Job insecurity Building the tool that may devalue one’s own expertise Some AI workers directly help improve systems that may automate a growing share of their own activity. Unlike many other professions, they do not necessarily discover this progress through the press. They may directly observe what models could not do a few months earlier and then take part in reducing the remaining limitations. Business Insider reports that several senior engineers seeing one therapist no longer imagine doing the same job in ten years. This uncertainty appears to concern something deeper than employment security alone. One therapist reports the following question among some patients: “I don't know who I am if my work gets automated.” The development also creates a paradox. AI can greatly increase an engineer’","url":"en/reading/ai-workers-psychosocial-risks/"},{"title":"AI Psychosocial Risks at Work | Prevention Framework","description":"Identify six Gollac psychosocial-risk factors and three AI-specific mechanisms, their organisational causes and possible occupational-health effects.","keywords":"Psychosocial risks · Organisation · Health AI becomes a risk when work organisation creates exposure. A model alone does not explain a health effect. Risk develops when the use of AI changes workload, autonomy, relationships, work quality or professional security without adequate resources for regulation and control. See the nine factors ↓ Assess a project Collective analysis Real work No individual diagnosis When the balance breaks Thomas Cole Destruction · 1836 The city’s apparent order collapses when its supports can no longer hold. The work reminds us that, at work, an equilibrium can quickly become fragile when workload, accountability, routes for challenge or skills are not followed with care. 06 + 03 Nine angles of analysis. Six Gollac families, supplemented by three exploratory axes specific to AI use. CAUSE Examine the organisation. A score, error or recommendation becomes hazardous only within a particular work situation. HEALTH Think in terms of exposure. Duration, frequency, combined constraints, available resources and room for action shape the level of risk. 01 / What creates exposure Four organisational choices to examine first. Start not with “is AI dangerous?”, but with who does what, under which targets, with what authority given to the system and what real means to regain control. 01 Targets raised too quickly. Theoretical time savings become higher volumes or shorter deadlines before the actual time required for checking is known. May drive: intensity, interruptions, work spilling beyond hours. 02 Decisions removed from real work. The tool is selected without involving users, worker representatives or those who will handle errors and difficult cases. May drive: loss of autonomy, conflict, workarounds. 03 Responsibility without resources. Workers must approve outputs without enough time, sources or authority to challenge them. May drive: cognitive overload, anxiety, impossible accountability. 04 Unclear professional change. Tasks are removed or recombined without training pathways, skill maintenance or credible information about jobs. May drive: insecurity, disengagement, skill erosion. 02 / Gollac framework The six families of psychosocial risk factors. These factors describe employment and organisational conditions that may affect health. They are neither symptoms nor diagnoses. G01 Work intensity and time. Volume, deadlines, interruptions, hours and complexity. With AI: raised targets, invisible correction work, permanent alerts. G02 Emotional demands. Managing one’s emotions, distress and conflict. With AI: announcing, explaining o","url":"en/risks-prevention/"},{"title":"Occupational risks related to AI","description":"Understand AI-related psychosocial, public-health, economic and social risks through two complementary scales of analysis.","keywords":"OCCUPATIONAL RISKS · REAL WORK · PREVENTION What are the risks of artificial intelligence at work? Risk does not come from the technology alone. It appears when its use transforms workload, decision-making latitude, monitoring, responsibilities, skills or job security without the means of control and regulation changing accordingly. AI can improve how a task is performed while degrading the conditions in which it is carried out. The main risks concern work intensification, the burden of verification, reduced autonomy, monitoring, the opacity of certain decisions, skill erosion, inequalities and professional uncertainty. Their significance depends on the use case, the role given to the system, the duration of exposure and the real possibilities of understanding, challenging or correcting how it works. Explore psychosocial risks → Assess and deploy a project → 01 / MAP Six families of risk to examine together. A single use can affect several dimensions of work at once. Analysis must therefore cover the work situation as a whole rather than a succession of risks considered separately. 01 / WORKLOAD Intensification and invisible work Raised targets, shortened deadlines or an increased volume of output may come with new checking, correction and coordination tasks that sometimes remain barely visible. 02 / POWER Autonomy, monitoring and contestation A recommendation, a score or an automated decision can become difficult to challenge when its criteria are opaque or when the avenues of appeal remain limited. 03 / ACCOUNTABILITY Answering for a decision without controlling it A person may remain responsible for a result produced with the help of a system without having the time, the information, the skills or the authority needed to verify its reliability. 04 / OCCUPATION Skills, learning and meaning at work Automating certain tasks can reduce the opportunities to practise, understand and pass on know-how, to the point of creating a gradual dependence on the tool. 05 / EQUITY Bias, data and inequalities A system’s errors or performance differences can unequally affect certain occupations, populations or situations that are insufficiently represented in the data. 06 / FUTURE Employment and job security Uncertainty about how tasks, occupations, assessment criteria or the value of skills will change can produce effects well before any job is actually cut. 02 / CONNECT From the system to the mechanism, then to the observable signal. This table offers reference points for connecting a use of AI to the transformations it can produce and to the signals that may appear i","url":"en/risks/"},{"title":"Economic and social risk linked to AI","description":"How AI could reduce the economic role of human labour, and which early signals occupational health can observe today.","keywords":"← Both risk pathways ECONOMIC AND SOCIAL RISK · PROSPECTIVE SCENARIO When human labour gradually ceases to be indispensable The risk is not limited to the number of jobs eliminated. It also concerns the place human labour retains in value creation, learning and economic power. Caution This pathway examines a possible systemic risk. It does not describe a demonstrated consequence of current AI. 01 · Assist 02 · Recommend 03 · Decide by default 04 · Act autonomously STARTING POINT Starting point This pathway examines a possible systemic risk. It does not describe a demonstrated consequence of current AI. As models perform more intellectual tasks, use software, make decisions and act autonomously, organisations can progressively replace part of human labour with capital, compute and AI models. The shift need not happen suddenly: it can occur one task, occupation and decision at a time. 01 From copilot to economic infrastructure AI is first introduced as a productivity tool. Its role can then move from assisting to recommending, deciding by default and acting autonomously. As human intervention begins to look slower or more expensive, competitive pressure can turn a technology choice into an economic necessity. READING POINT Observe what this change means for work, skills, autonomy and collective prevention. 02 An economy in which labour matters less Work is both a source of income and a form of economic power. If a larger share of value came from capital-intensive AI infrastructure, consequences could include weaker bargaining power, fewer entry-level learning routes and greater concentration of economic power. What happens to a society when the main economic contribution of most of its members gradually becomes less necessary? READING POINT Observe what this change means for work, skills, autonomy and collective prevention. 03 The ‘intelligence curse’: when incentives change The authors draw an analogy with the resource curse. If organisations and states derive progressively more value from automated systems than from employees, incentives to invest in human capital could change. This is a systemic-risk scenario, not a demonstrated consequence of current AI. READING POINT Observe what this change means for work, skills, autonomy and collective prevention. 04 And before unemployment? The first signals may appear in work organisation: fewer junior positions, tasks removed from roles, higher productivity expectations, reduced discretion, greater monitoring, altered staffing ratios, shrinking learning opportunities and uncertainty about career pathways. READIN","url":"en/risks/economic-social/"},{"title":"Understanding AI at Work","description":"A clear guide to workplace AI systems, large language models, their capabilities and limits, and how to interpret current AI benchmarks.","keywords":"Understand · Essential concepts Understand artificial intelligence at work. Distinguish between systems, understand how a language model produces an answer, and read performance results without making them say more than they do. Start reading ↓ Choose a resource 12-minute read Selected sources Updated August 2026 What seems stable Thomas Cole The Savage State · 1834 The landscape still seems open, while the mountain gives the impression of a stable landmark. The work reminds us that an apparent equilibrium can quickly become fragile when the setting changes: understanding an AI system means identifying what must remain under control — the task, the data and the decisions — before novelty hides the weak points. View the artwork ↗ 01 “AI” does not describe a single tool. Generating text, assigning a score and allocating tasks are three different functions, with different risks. 02 An LLM produces a plausible continuation. It calculates probabilities from context; it does not independently verify whether its answer is true or compliant. 03 Real work is the right level of analysis. The model matters, but so do the task, the data, the people, the decision rules and the organisation. 01 / Name the system First, what kind of artificial intelligence are we talking about? The term “AI” covers systems that do not produce the same kind of output. To understand a professional use, begin by identifying what the tool actually does. 01 · Generate Generative AI It creates or transforms text, images, sound or code from an instruction. An LLM-based conversational assistant belongs to this family. Output New content Watch for Accuracy, sources, input data 02 · Predict Predictive AI It estimates a probability, assigns a score or classifies a case using past data: default risk, case priority or diagnostic support. Output A score or category Watch for Bias, thresholds, false positives and false negatives 03 · Organise Algorithmic management It allocates tasks, sets processing priorities, measures activity or influences a management decision. It may or may not include generative AI. Output An instruction or decision Watch for Autonomy, appeal routes, work intensification 02 / Inside an LLM How a language model produces an answer. A large language model — or LLM — is a neural network trained to predict what comes next in a text. It does not retrieve a ready-made answer from a database: it composes one, unit by unit. 01 · Tokenise Text becomes tokens The prompt and supplied documents are split into small units: words, word fragments or symbols. 02 · Represent Tokens become numbe","url":"en/understand/"},{"title":"AI in Occupational Health Services and France’s PST 5","description":"What France’s Occupational Health Plan 2026–2030 says about AI at work, practical AI uses in occupational health services and results from INRS study TF 335.","keywords":"OHS services · Professional uses · Human oversight AI can support an OHS service when the task is precise. Reviewing a document, finding a source, structuring observations or preparing training: value does not come from “AI” in general, but from a limited use that can be tested and corrected. An evaluated example. A study published by INRS in June 2026 tested a language model as a second reader of occupational physician recommendations. It shows genuine potential, but not an ability to replace the occupational physician. See the study and results ↓ Explore the uses Limited task Human reference Decision retained Between two sides Thomas Cole The Oxbow · 1836 Between the storm and the valley, the landscape rests in an uncertain balance. The work reminds us that every promising use must be considered from both sides: what it improves and what it may make fragile if its effects are not observed. View the painting at The Met ↗ PST 5 · 2026–2030 France’s national plan makes AI at work an occupational health issue. Under Objective 3, France’s Occupational Health Plan 2026–2030 states that AI-related transformations should be introduced through consultation, protect working conditions and be assessed through their real effects. Sub-action 3.1.1 provides for a national Observatory for AI at Work, led by Anact. 01 Investigate both sides. PST 5 calls for evidence on AI as a tool for preventing occupational risks and improving working conditions, while also examining AI systems and their uses as possible sources of occupational risk. 02 Produce evidence that supports action. The plan provides for quantitative and qualitative studies, experiments, training, webinars, and practical tools and methods that can turn findings into prevention measures. 03 Give OHS services a defined role. Annex 3 calls them “pivotal actors”. It asks them to contribute evidence on AI with Présanse and Anact, while adopting a measured use of AI in their own activities. For an inter-company occupational health service (SPSTI), the task is therefore twofold: test clearly bounded professional uses with care, and observe what AI deployment changes in client organisations — workload, autonomy, skills, teams and occupational risks. Source: France’s Occupational Health Plan 2026–2030, Action 3.1.1, pp. 44–45, and Annex 3, pp. 154–156. Read the official document (French) ↗ 01 / Where AI may help Six concrete uses with clearly defined human responsibility. Except for the review case presented below, these uses are possibilities to experiment with and assess in each service. 01 · Occupational medicine","url":"en/uses-and-field/occupational-health-example/"}];
